Sundial Chaal

Ingredients: DREAM POP & SHOEGAZE + BHANGRA + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Baritone Saxophone + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a saltwater-corroded 1958 magnetic tape found inside an unsealed lead time capsule off the coast of Goa, where sock-hop rockabilly, Punjabi harvesting chants, and humid ocean-mist degradation fused into a single ghostly recording.

Sundial Chaal sounds like a 1950s beachfront sock-hop submerged in warm, turquoise brine: thunderous Punjabi dhol barrels galloping in swinging triplet chaal rhythms, violently smothered in blinding walls of shoegaze fuzz, while a foghorn-heavy 1950s baritone saxophone honks greasy rhythm-and-blues licks beneath ethereal, sun-drenched four-part doo-wop harmonies floating through three-second plate reverberation.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 86–98 BPM felt in a hypnotic double-time lilt (172–196 BPM internal swing); the syncopated, bouncing 8-beat chaal triplet is grafted directly over a languid 1950s 6/8 slow-dance backbeat.
- Harmonic Palette: Classic 1950s doo-wop changes (I–vi–IV–V) warped into shimmering Major 7th, sus2, and add9 voicings, decorated with microtonal Punjabi Bilawal and Khamaj thahat vocal embellishments.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Oxide-Bloom Overdrive: Pushing preamps into severe tape saturation that mimics sun-baked, saline-crusted magnetic tape ribbons.
  2. Bari-Drone Bi-Amping: Routing a 1950s baritone saxophone simultaneously through a clean rotating Leslie horn and a heavily saturated fuzz-box to create a subterranean, wall-of-sound brass foundation.
  3. Ghungroo Gating: Percussive ankle-bell jingles fed into high-feedback reverse reverbs that bloom backward before snapping shut on every rimshot.
- Dynamics: Songs rise out of balmy, submerged ambient murmurs—tumbi strums dripping in spring reverb and cooing acapella hums—before instantly erupting into blinding, serotonin-drenched tidal waves of roaring white-noise guitars, galloping dhol strikes, and visceral baritone honks.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement flared to life across the sun-scorched sandbars of Daman and Diu when audio-restorers retrieved an unspooled 1958 lead cylinder from an offshore reef. The cassette was nearly ruined by marine bacteria and brine, fusing a forgotten Goan-Portuguese radio broadcast of American doo-wop with field recordings of migrant Punjabi farmhands into a swirling, distorted soup. Local music students didn't attempt to clean the audio; they treated the corrupted tape as a holy blueprint. The resulting subculture, nicknamed The Hour-Keepers, gathers at tropical dawn for open-air beachfront dances known as Pendulum Riots. Fashion blends mid-century seaside Americana with traditional Punjabi regalia: pastel-yellow camp-collar bowling shirts paired with pleated lungis, cat-eye horn-rimmed shades, and raw-silk turbans festooned with tarnished clock faces and brass gears. Their central ritual involves tossing marigolds directly into the bell of a roaring baritone sax while dancers lock shoulders to perform the Pendulum Giddha, swaying in sync with the dizzying, swung fuzz.

Typical Instruments: 1950s Conn 12M 'Naked Lady' Baritone Saxophone, twin-headed Punjabi Dhol (beaten with curved bamboo dagga and tili sticks), 1959 Fender Jazzmaster with multiple fuzz pedals, Punjabi Tumbi, Ghungroo (brass ankle bells), vintage Danelectro 6-string bass, Farfisa organ, and lush four-part coed vocal ensembles.

For Fans Of: My Bloody Valentine, Malkit Singh, The Flamingos, Dick Dale.

